abstract: >-
  In human-robot collaboration (HRC), task scheduling
  determines how assembly steps are assigned to agents.
  These schedules can be static, generated prior to
  execution, or dynamic, adapting to events in real-time.
  Despite the proliferation of novel scheduling algorithms,
  the robotics field lacks standardized, reproducible
  methods for evaluating how human collaborators experience
  these competing policies. Existing user studies suffer
  from heterogeneous hardware setups, proprietary software
  stacks, and unshared study materials. 

  We address this reproducibility gap by introducing a
  complete, open-source platform designed for HRC scheduling
  experiments. The provided artifact includes the hardware
  setup, graphical user interface, study documents, and a
  modular software architecture with a dedicated scheduler
  interface to test custom algorithms.

  Utilizing this platform, we conducted a within-subjects
  user study (n = 22) to compare static and dynamic task
  sharing during an assembly scenario with experimentally
  induced human absences. Our results demonstrate that
  dynamic scheduling significantly reduces the user's
  perceived workload and increases perceived team fluency,
  without raising mental demand.
